---
title: Cardholder Blocks and Status Changes
slug: guides/cardholder-blocks
description: |
  How account and card blocks work, which webhooks they produce, and how to
  keep block status accurate in your application.
---

Immersve uses blocks to restrict spending. A block applies at one of two
scopes: the cardholder account or an individual card. The two scopes behave
differently, produce different webhooks, and require different handling in
your application. This guide explains the block model and how to detect,
reconcile, and display block status.

## Block Scopes

{% table %}
* Scope
* Applies To
* Example Causes
* Webhook Topics
---
* Account
* The cardholder account and all spending under it
* Activation, KYC review, fraud review
* `cardholder-block-created`, `cardholder-block-released`
---
* Card
* A single card
* Cardholder freezes a card
* `card-block-created`, `card-block-released`
{% /table %}

## Account Blocks

An account block restricts the cardholder account as a whole. While an
account block is active the cardholder cannot transact on any card, and new
cards cannot be created for the account.

An account block does **not** modify the cardholder's card records. The
{% link endpoint="get-card" /%} endpoint continues to return
`"status": "active"` for cards under a blocked account. Do not infer that a
cardholder can spend from card status alone. See the Card View Back-Stop
section below.

Account blocks are reflected by the
{% link endpoint="get-spending-prerequisites" /%} endpoint: while a block is
active, the prerequisite stages report `"status": "blocked"`.

When an account block is created, a {% link title="Cardholder Block Created"
endpoint="webhook-topics/cardholder-block-created" /%} webhook is sent:

```json {% title="Example cardholder-block-created webhook payload." %}
{
  "blockId": "75a7e8ef0d234d0a6bf755e07d39eb03",
  "cardholderAccountId": "9b7552cf9abab0f2efed72df6b3ce4bc",
  "reason": "kyc"
}
```

When the block is released, a matching {% link title="Cardholder Block Released"
endpoint="webhook-topics/cardholder-block-released" /%} webhook is sent with
the same fields.

### Block Reasons

The `reason` field identifies why the block was placed and determines how
your application should respond.

{% table %}
* Reason
* Description
* Suggested Treatment
---
* activation
* Placed when the cardholder account is created, and released once all
  activation prerequisites are satisfied. See {% link page="guides/cardholder-activation" /%}.
* Present onboarding progress. Treat the release as the cardholder becoming
  active.
---
* kyc
* Placed when identity verification requires further review or has failed.
* Direct the cardholder to any outstanding actions returned by {% link
  endpoint="get-spending-prerequisites" /%}. See {% link page="guides/kyc-spending-prerequisites" /%}.
---
* fraud
* Placed while the account is under review.
* Present a neutral message, such as "your card is temporarily unavailable",
  and direct the cardholder to support. Do not present review specifics.
{% /table %}

## Card Blocks

A card block restricts a single card and leaves the rest of the account
unaffected. The most common card block is a cardholder-initiated freeze via
the {% link endpoint="freeze-card" /%} endpoint. Card blocks update the card
record and produce the {% link title="Card Block Created"
endpoint="webhook-topics/card-block-created" /%} and {% link title="Card Block Released"
endpoint="webhook-topics/card-block-released" /%} webhooks. See
{% link page="guides/card-lifecycle" /%} for card states and transitions.

## Detecting Block Changes

Webhooks are the recommended way to keep block status current in your
application.

1. **Configure a Webhook Listener** –
   Set up and register your webhook listener. See
   {% link page="guides/configuring-webhook-listeners" /%} for configuration,
   delivery, retry, and deduplication behavior.

2. **Subscribe to the Block Topics** –
   Subscribe your listener to `cardholder-block-created`,
   `cardholder-block-released`, `card-block-created`, and
   `card-block-released`.

3. **Route on Scope and Reason** –
   Apply `cardholder-block-*` notifications to the account identified by
   `cardholderAccountId`, using `reason` to select the treatment described
   above. Apply `card-block-*` notifications to the card identified in the
   payload.

## Reconciling Block Status

Webhooks provide the real-time signal. The
{% link endpoint="get-cardholder-blocks" /%} endpoint is the authoritative
record of an account's blocks, including each block's status and reason. Call
it to reconcile your stored state, for example after recovering from an
outage that outlasted webhook retries.

### Card View Back-Stop

When a cardholder opens a card view in your application, refresh both of the
following:

- <wbr/>{% link endpoint="get-spending-prerequisites" /%} – reflects account
  blocks.
- <wbr/>{% link endpoint="get-card" /%} – reflects card blocks.

Treat a blocked prerequisite stage as authoritative over an active card
status. Because account blocks do not modify card records, checking the card
record alone will not detect an account block.
